@page rh="BACK MATTER · SOURCES AND EDITIONS · 2 OF 5 · HEBREW BIBLE" m="PART I; DOSSIERS I–IV; FOCUSED STUDIES; PART II"
# Texts, lexicons and commentaries
rec:
MT | *Biblia Hebraica Stuttgartensia*. Stuttgart: Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft. The standard printed Masoretic text. Cite the printing you use.
LXX | Rahlfs–Hanhart, *Septuaginta*. Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft. Consulted where the Greek differs from the Hebrew.
DSS | Dead Sea Scroll manuscripts cited by siglum, such as 4QDeut and 4Q365.
BDB | Brown, Driver and Briggs, *A Hebrew and English Lexicon of the Old Testament*. Oxford: Clarendon, 1906.
HALOT | Koehler and Baumgartner, *The Hebrew and Aramaic Lexicon of the Old Testament*. Leiden: Brill. The current standard. Use it over BDB where they conflict.
Milgrom | Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus*. Anchor Bible 3, 3A, 3B. New York: Doubleday, 1991–2001.
Klawans | Jonathan Klawans, *Impurity and Sin in Ancient Judaism*. Oxford University Press, 2000.
Douglas | Mary Douglas, *Purity and Danger*. Routledge, 1966, and *Leviticus as Literature*. Oxford, 1999.
Olyan | Saul M. Olyan, *Disability in the Hebrew Bible*. Cambridge University Press, 2008.
Bergsma | John Sietze Bergsma, *The Jubilee from Leviticus to Qumran*. Leiden: Brill, 2007.
Weinfeld | Moshe Weinfeld, *Deuteronomy 1–11*. Anchor Bible 5. Doubleday, 1991, and *Deuteronomy and the Deuteronomic School*. Oxford: Clarendon, 1972.
Levinson | Bernard M. Levinson, *Deuteronomy and the Hermeneutics of Legal Innovation*. Oxford University Press, 1997.
Goldenberg | David M. Goldenberg, *The Curse of Ham*. Princeton University Press, 2003.

@page rh="BACK MATTER · SOURCES AND EDITIONS · 2 OF 5 · HEBREW BIBLE, CONTINUED" m="PART I; DOSSIERS I–IV; FOCUSED STUDIES; PART II"
# Texts, lexicons and commentaries, continued
rec:
Haynes | Stephen R. Haynes, *Noah's Curse*. Oxford University Press, 2002.
Levenson | Jon D. Levenson, *The Death and Resurrection of the Beloved Son*. Yale University Press, 1993.
Trible | Phyllis Trible, *God and the Rhetoric of Sexuality* (1978) and *Texts of Terror* (1984). Fortress.
Williams | Delores S. Williams, *Sisters in the Wilderness*. Orbis, 1993.
Westermann · Alter · Propp · Childs · Meyers · Japhet · Williamson · Blenkinsopp · Fox · Seow · Greenberg · Nogalski · Sweeney | The commentaries named in the focused-study and dossier margins. Record the editions you own on the last page.

@page rh="BACK MATTER · SOURCES AND EDITIONS · 3 OF 5 · NEW TESTAMENT AND COMPARATIVE" m="PART III; PART IV; GOSPEL GRID"
# Greek texts, Jesus research, ancient neighbors
rec:
NA28 | *Novum Testamentum Graece*, 28th revised edition. Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft, 2012. Where the companion says a reading is absent from the oldest manuscripts, this is the apparatus it means.
UBS5 | *The Greek New Testament*, 5th revised edition. United Bible Societies, 2014.
Sanders | E. P. Sanders, *The Historical Figure of Jesus*. Penguin, 1993.
Ehrman | Bart D. Ehrman, *The New Testament: A Historical Introduction*. Oxford University Press. Record your edition, since page references move.
Goodacre | Mark Goodacre, *The Case Against Q*. Trinity Press International, 2002.
Borg and Crossan | Marcus Borg and John Dominic Crossan, *The Last Week*. HarperOne, 2006.
Levine | Amy-Jill Levine, *The Misunderstood Jew*. HarperOne, 2006.
Epp | Eldon Jay Epp, *Junia: The First Woman Apostle*. Fortress, 2005.
COS | William W. Hallo and K. Lawson Younger, eds., *The Context of Scripture*. Brill, 1997–2002. The flood parallels, the Mesha inscription and the law codes.
SAA 2 | Simo Parpola and Kazuko Watanabe, *Neo-Assyrian Treaties and Loyalty Oaths*. Helsinki University Press, 1988. The succession-treaty curses compared with Deuteronomy 28.
Steymans | Hans Ulrich Steymans, *Deuteronomium 28 und die adê zur Thronfolgeregelung Asarhaddons*. 1995.
Mendenhall · Moran | George Mendenhall, "Covenant Forms in Israelite Tradition" (1954–55). William Moran, "The Ancient Near Eastern Background of the Love of God in Deuteronomy" (1963).

@page rh="BACK MATTER · SOURCES AND EDITIONS · 4 OF 5 · AMERICAN HISTORY" m="PART IV, V; LINKS TO CLICK THROUGH AT PROOFING"
# Behind Part IV's American history
small: These were checked against search results. The pages themselves still need a click-through at proofing.
rec:
Winthrop | Wikisource, *A Model of Christian Charity* · Teaching American History, "A City upon a Hill" · Museum of the Bible, "A City Upon a Hill" · Rodgers, *As a City on a Hill* (2018)
Slavery and the Bible | Furman University, Richard Furman's *Exposition* · Wake Forest, "Defending the Indefensible" · HathiTrust, Stringfellow's *Brief Examination* · Grimké, *Appeal to the Christian Women of the South* · Douglass, *Narrative*, Appendix · Noll, *The Civil War as a Theological Crisis* (2006)
The Southern Baptist Convention | *Christianity Today* on the 1995 resolution · the SBC Racial Reconciliation Resolution · Baptist News Global
The 1807 Slave Bible | NPR, December 9, 2018 · Wikipedia, *Select Parts of the Holy Bible*
The Black church | Raboteau, *Slave Religion* (1978) · Lincoln and Mamiya, *The Black Church in the African American Experience* (1990) · Wikipedia, Richard Allen and Emanuel AME · BlackPast · Equal Justice Initiative, July 2, 1822
Christian nationalism | PRRI and Brookings, February 2023 · Whitehead and Perry, *Taking America Back for God* (2020) · Mark David Hall · Wolfe, *The Case for Christian Nationalism* (2022)
Reconstructionism | Rushdoony, *The Institutes of Biblical Law* (1973) · Bahnsen, *Theonomy in Christian Ethics* (1977) · Barker and Godfrey, eds., *Theonomy: A Reformed Critique* (1990) · Chalcedon
Dominionism and the Seven Mountains | Diamond, *Spiritual Warfare* (1989) · Northeastern University, November 15, 2022 · Wikipedia, Seven Mountain Mandate · Good Faith Media · *Christian Post* · Taylor, *The Violent Take It by Force* (2024)
Danvers and CBE | Wikipedia, Council on Biblical Manhood and Womanhood · CBE, "A Brief History" · *Christianity Today*, July 2011

